---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
import { breadcrumbLd } from '../lib/seo';
import scales from '../data/scales.json';

const title = `About ${site.name}`;
const description = `How ${site.name} calculates grades and GPA: the exact formulas, where the grading-scale data comes from, and how to verify it.`;
const lastUpdated = '2026-09-23';
const scaleCount = (scales as unknown as { generic?: boolean }[]).length;
const verifiedCount = (scales as unknown as { generic?: boolean }[]).filter((s) => !s.generic).length;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} jsonLd={breadcrumbLd([{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about/' }])}>
  <article class="container container--narrow section prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about/' }]} />
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="muted small">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>
    <p>
      {site.name} is a set of four small calculators for students: a final grade calculator, a weighted grade book, a GPA calculator and a GPA planner. Every result is
      computed in your browser from the numbers you type — nothing is uploaded, and there is no account.
    </p>

    <h2>Final grade formula</h2>
    <p>
      Your course grade is a weighted average of your current grade and the final: <code>grade = current × (1 − weight) + final × weight</code>, where{' '}
      <code>weight</code> is the final's share of the course grade (30% = 0.30). Solving for the final score that hits your target gives:
    </p>
    <pre tabindex="0">final = (target − current × (1 − weight)) / weight</pre>
    <p>
      A result above 100% is flagged "not possible" (assuming no extra credit); a result at or below 0% is flagged "already secured." The three variants work the same
      way with adjusted algebra:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Final replaces lowest test</strong> — the final is added to your list of test scores as one more test, and the lowest of that combined list is dropped
        before averaging. If the required final would itself be the lowest score, your grade is already decided and doesn't depend on the final.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two-part final</strong> — the final's total weight is split across two components; if you already know part 1's score, the calculator solves only for
        part 2.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Points-based</strong> — for courses graded on raw points rather than percentages: <code>needed points = target% × (points possible + final points) − points earned</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Weighted grade book</h2>
    <p>
      Each category's average is either points-weighted (<code>sum of points earned ÷ sum of points possible</code>) or an equal-weight average of each item's
      percentage — your choice. Within a category, the lowest N scores are dropped (N = "drop lowest"), always keeping at least one graded item. Categories with
      no graded items yet are excluded from the course grade and the remaining categories' weights are renormalised to sum to 100% — the same convention most
      learning-management systems (Canvas, PowerSchool, etc.) use for an in-progress "current grade." "What-if" rows are excluded from the current grade but included
      in the "with what-ifs" projection.
    </p>

    <h2>GPA formula</h2>
    <p>
      Semester GPA is quality points divided by credits: <code>Σ(grade points × credits) ÷ Σ(credits)</code>, counting only courses with both a graded, GPA-eligible
      grade and non-zero credits. Cumulative GPA folds in a prior GPA and credit count:
    </p>
    <pre tabindex="0">cumulative = (priorGPA × priorCredits + thisSemester'sQualityPoints) / (priorCredits + thisSemester'sCredits)</pre>
    <p>
      When Honors/AP weighting is enabled on a scale that supports it, the configured bonus (for example +0.5 for Honors, +1.0 for AP/IB) is added to the grade point
      before multiplying by credits — never to a failing grade.
    </p>
    <p>
      See <a href={withBase('/how-to-calculate-gpa/')}>how to calculate GPA</a> for a fully worked example, or <a href={withBase('/weighted-vs-unweighted-gpa/')}>weighted
      vs. unweighted GPA</a> for a side-by-side comparison.
    </p>

    <h2>GPA planner</h2>
    <p>
      The planner rearranges the cumulative-GPA formula to solve for the average you need: <code>requiredAverage = (target × (credits + newCredits) − GPA × credits) / newCredits</code>,
      and for the credits needed at a given average: <code>credits = currentCredits × (target − GPA) / (average − target)</code> (undefined/negative when that average can
      never reach the target).
    </p>

    <h2>Grading-scale data</h2>
    <p>
      GradeGoal ships {scaleCount} grading scales ({verifiedCount} tied to a specific, cited institution or national system; the rest are widely used generic
      conventions such as the standard unweighted 4.0 scale). Every scale page states whether it's generic or sourced, links the official page it was verified
      against, and shows the verification date. Grading policies change and vary by department — always check your own syllabus or registrar page for figures that
      matter for your transcript. Scale data lives in <code>src/data/scales.json</code> in the site's source and is never fetched at runtime.
    </p>

    <h2>Rounding</h2>
    <p>
      Internal math uses full floating-point precision; only the final displayed number is rounded (to 1–3 decimal places depending on the field), using
      round-half-away-from-zero to avoid the classic <code>1.005 → 1.00</code> floating-point artefact.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy by design</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name} runs entirely in your browser. Inputs are not uploaded, no account is needed, and there are no tracking cookies. Saved courses and semesters use
      your browser's local storage only — see the <a href={withBase('/privacy/')}>privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who maintains it</h2>
    <p>
      Built and maintained by <a href={site.author.url} rel="noopener">{site.author.name}</a>. Launched {site.launched}. Found an error in a grading scale, a bug, or
      have a suggestion? <a href={withBase('/contact/')}>Get in touch</a> — data corrections are especially welcome, with a source we can verify.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
